Hi Peter, hi Gordon,

the reason for the fold change computation in SAM is simply that Tusher et
al. also use these fold changes. In 2002, SAM was part of my diploma thesis
and I tried to reproduce the results of Tusher et al. and the results that
are provided by the Excel SAM version. Since then many things in sam have
changed, mainly to make sam faster, more user-friendly and more flexible.
But I didn't change the computation of the fold change to actually avoid the
question why the fold changes differ between the siggenes SAM and the Excel
SAM. 

Admittedly, "because Tusher et al. do so" is not a very good reason, but I
have stopped counting how often I was ask why this and that differs between
Excel SAM and siggenes SAM.

Would it help you if I add another slot to the SAM object that contains the
numerator of the test statistics? Note that you can compute these numerators
by 

> sam.out at d * (sam.out at s0 + sam.out at s)

if sam.out is the output of sam.

